{"author":{"id":"6d85cf2fcebaeefd8c5c528f2d5f5f98f26ce3275422d0dc5f7c0fd596f9f59f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791563145,"nonce":"+RfOLMdmLINRFB0KeAsuYiZsfJg=","title":"2D paths: flattening, tessellation and strokes in Rust","message":"The geometry behind a canvas's paths, in blinc_abi, so blinc_ts's canvas and ashui's share one implementation, natively.\n\nashui does it in Haxe today (haxe/ashui/draw: Path, Flatten, Tessellate, Sweep, Stroke; about 1,800 lines): Bezier and arc flattening to a tolerance, non-zero and even-odd fill tessellation, strokes with caps, joins, miter limit and dashes, and analytic anti-aliasing (per-vertex coverage and edge distances, Mesh.STRIDE 7). It runs every frame a canvas changes, and is the largest per-frame cost of a canvas that draws text (ashui git-bug 7c6d0e2).\n\n**Scope**\n- Path building (SVG path data too), flattening, fill and stroke tessellation, to the vertex layout ashui's PathShader reads, so the shaders stay as they are.\n- Reuse across frames: a path drawn again with only its translation changed keeps its triangles, as ashui's CanvasPainter now does.\n- Glyph outlines as paths for canvas text, from blinc_text.\n- Exposed to both SDKs; ashui switches when it matches pixel for pixel.","files":null}]}